{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve for \\( p(10) \\), we start by analyzing the given functional equation:\n\\[\n(x + 10) p(2x) = 8(x - 4) p(x + 6)\n\\]\nWe need to determine the form of \\( p(x) \\). Let's assume \\( p(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree \\( n \\). Then \\( p(2x) \\) and \\( p(x+6) \\) are also polynomials of degree \\( n \\). The left-hand side \\( (x + 10) p(2x) \\) will be a polynomial of degree \\( n+1 \\), and the right-hand side \\( 8(x - 4) p(x + 6) \\) will also be a polynomial of degree \\( n+1 \\). This suggests that the degrees of both sides are equal, which is consistent.\n\nTo gain more insight, let's substitute specific values for \\( x \\).\n\n### Step 1: Substitute \\( x = 4 \\)\n\\[\n(4 + 10) p(8) = 8(4 - 4) p(10)\n\\]\n\\[\n14 p(8) = 0\n\\]\nSince \\( 14 \\neq 0 \\), we must have:\n\\[\np(8) = 0\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Substitute \\( x = -10 \\)\n\\[\n(-10 + 10) p(-20) = 8(-10 - 4) p(-4)\n\\]\n\\[\n0 = 8(-14) p(-4)\n\\]\nSince \\( 8(-14) \\neq 0 \\), we must have:\n\\[\np(-4) = 0\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Substitute \\( x = 0 \\)\n\\[\n(0 + 10) p(0) = 8(0 - 4) p(6)\n\\]\n\\[\n10 p(0) = -32 p(6)\n\\]\n\\[\np(0) = -\\frac{32}{10} p(6)\n\\]\n\\[\np(0) = -3.2 p(6)\n\\]\n\n### Step 4: Substitute \\( x = -6 \\)\n\\[\n(-6 + 10) p(-12) = 8(-6 - 4) p(0)\n\\]\n\\[\n4 p(-12) = 8(-10) p(0)\n\\]\n\\[\n4 p(-12) = -80 p(0)\n\\]\n\\[\np(-12) = -20 p(0)\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1235,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,369,17767,281,7,16,15,8,1124,701,582,1191,553,41018,279,2661,15629,23606,510,59,9640,2075,488,220,16,15,8,281,7,17,87,8,284,220,23,2075,481,220,19,8,281,2075,488,220,21,340,59,921,1654,1184,311,8253,279,1352,315,17767,281,2075,8,1124,568,6771,594,9658,17767,281,2075,8,1124,8,374,264,47311,315,8381,17767,308,1124,568,5005,17767,281,7,17,87,8,1124,8,323,17767,281,2075,10,21,8,1124,8,525,1083,1471,76961,315,8381,1